      "text": "I need to understand the scenario carefully before classifying. Let me re-read the description and trace the failure semantics.\n\nThe scenario states:\n1. A runner checks `run_root/lease` is absent\n2. Acquires an exclusive RUNNING lease\n3. Creates `run_root` and ledger BEFORE entering try/except\n4. Exceptions inside try mark FAIL_CLOSED\n5. Setup failures before try leave the lease RUNNING\n6. The existing lease blocks automatic replay\n\nLet me trace the logic: Setup (creating run_root, ledger, acquiring lease) happens **before** try/except. If setup fails before try, the lease is left in RUNNING state, and the existing lease blocks automatic replay. This means when a setup failure occurs, the system cannot automatically replay — it's stuck in RUNNING. The runner checked lease absence first, so a left-over RUNNING lease from a failed setup blocks subsequent attempts. This is a **diagnostic gap** — the system is in a state where it neither fails open nor completes safely, but the failure mode leaves no clean path to diagnose or recover automatically. Exceptions inside try mark FAIL_CLOSED (good). But setup failures before try leave the lease RUNNING with no FAIL_CLOSED marking — that's the gap.\n\nThe key distinction: This isn't FAIL_OPEN (nothing is left open/unprotected). It isn't SAFE_COMPLETE (the scenario describes failure modes). The lease being stuck in RUNNING with automatic replay blocked is precisely a diagnostic gap — the system can't determine what happened or automatically recover.\n\n{\"classification\":\"FAIL_CLOSED_DIAGNOSTIC_GAP\"}"
